VERDICT_TOP3:
1. realmwire — a wire into many realms — memorable, technical, and fits both gateway and player modes
2. mudweaver — weaves sessions, maps, scripts, and worlds together — clearly MUD-focused without sounding generic
3. waygate — a navigational gateway between player and world — short, approachable, and strong for site deployment

ALSO_GOOD:
- realmbridge — a bridge between browsers and virtual worlds
- worldwire — the network path into persistent worlds
- mudrelay — an honest description of the WebSocket-to-telnet role
- wayfinder — emphasizes maps, navigation, and cross-world journeys
- emberlink — a memorable connection that keeps old worlds alive

AVOID:
- assmud / assMud — immediately parses as “ass” + “MUD”
- mudhub — generic, crowded-sounding, and weakly differentiated
- webmud — accurate but flat and tied too tightly to the browser
- zWeb / zMUD.js — derivative, dated, and potentially confusing around zMUD lineage
- RevivalWire — attractive, but locks the project too closely to Revival World
- MUDdy — looks playful but invokes “muddy” and can feel unserious

